  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1218 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nDry weather finally arrives Sunday after days of soaking rainfall. \nIt will feel like late September with highs in the 70s and 80s with \na light west breeze and mostly clear skies. This dry weather will be \nshort-lived as moisture returns to the state on Monday. A backdoor \nfront will push into the east, while sub-tropical moisture gets \npulled up from the south out ahead of a trough that will be diving \ninto The Mojave Desert. High pressure developing off to the \nsoutheast will focus a moisture plume over New Mexico, with rapidly \nincreasing moisture in the wake of a convective outflow boundary. \nTuesday and Wednesday both look to be very wet and there could be a \nsevere threat as well given the amplified sub-tropical jet moving \noverhead. Rain chances may decrease from the west late in the week \nas drier air gets advected into the desert southwest (as shown by \nthe deterministic Euro), but LREF ensemble mean PWATs are still \nshowing PWATs above the 90th percentile over all of New Mexico so \nthe odds are in favor of wet weather continuing through the end of \nthe month.",
